hasValidPositions :: Value -> Bool
  ! fromJSON-decodes the Value as Player and checks allPositions is Just non-empty
  ! called by FromJSON TeamData to filter players before they enter the players map
  ! duplicated identically in Validators.hasValidPositions and Input_trace.hasValidPositions

%decision PlayerGameIdBackfilledAfterParse
  what:    FromJSON Player sets gameid = Nothing; Scraper.assignGameIdToPlayers populates it later
  why:     GameId is at the top of the boxscore JSON, not inside individual player records. Threading parent context through FromJSON would require a custom parser; backfill is structurally simpler. If a code path forgets the backfill, downstream code sees Nothing and (in playerToJsonPlayerData) emits empty-string Map keys.
  affects: 

%decision GameIdGameDateBackfilledAfterParse
  what:    FromJSON GameID sets game_date = Nothing; Scraper.assignDateToSchedule populates it later
  why:     Same reason as Player.gameid backfill: the date is at a higher level in the schedule JSON than the GameID record. Backfill runs once per fetched schedule via assignDateToSchedule.
  affects: 

%decision TeamDataFiltersInvalidPlayers
  what:    FromJSON TeamData drops players whose allPositions is empty or absent
  why:     hasValidPositions checks allPositions per-player and returns False for empty/missing lists. Filtered players never appear in the resulting M.Map.
  affects: hasValidPositions

%decision ActiveRosterMetadataOptional
  what:    ActiveRoster's dataPulled and checksum are Maybe Text
  why:     Source MLB API does not include these fields; Scraper.writeRosterToFile adds them on write. Asymmetric with OfficialRoster which requires both.
  affects: 

%decision EveryStatFieldIsMaybe
  what:    BattingStats has 25 Maybe Int fields; PitchingStats has 45 fields, all Maybe Int except pit_inningsPitched :: Maybe Text
  why:     MLB Stats API omits fields when the corresponding event didn't occur in the game. Maybe Int over default-zero pushes the 'absent' vs 'zero' distinction to the consumer; in practice every consumer uses fromMaybe 0. FromJSON uses .:? on every field so any subset can be absent.
  affects: 

%decision InningsPitchedIsTextNotDouble
  what:    pit_inningsPitched :: Maybe Text
  why:     MLB sends innings as '6.2' meaning 6 and 2/3 innings - the decimal is a sub-third count, not a tenth. Storing as Text preserves the notation for display; PointCalc.calcPitchingPoints parses to Double via readMaybe, which silently treats '6.2' as 6.2 (not 6.6666...). BUG: this is wrong by up to 0.06 IP per pitcher per game and affects quality_start gating.
  affects: 

%decision ActivePlayerNestedFieldExtraction
  what:    FromJSON ActivePlayer pulls currentTeam, primaryPosition, batSide, pitchHand from nested objects via traverse (.: 'id') or traverse (.: 'code')
  why:     MLB API returns these as objects with embedded code/id fields; the Haskell type flattens them to Maybe Text/Maybe Int. If the nested object exists but lacks the inner field, the parse fails (does not silently become Nothing).
  affects: 

%decision PlayerNormalizesEmptyAllPositions
  what:    FromJSON Player treats allPositions = Just [] identically to allPositions = Nothing
  why:     Normalization happens at parse time so consumers don't need to differentiate 'absent' from 'empty list'. hasValidPositions then returns False for both cases.
  affects: hasValidPositions


